<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Matsumae Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1604 CE – 1871 CE · capital Matsumae (Fukuyama)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The northernmost domain of Tokugawa Japan, which held a monopoly on trade with the Ainu and controlled southern Hokkaido.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Kakizaki family had established themselves on the southern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hokkaido">Hokkaido</a> in the 15th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsumae-yoshihiro">Kakizaki Yoshihiro</a>, who took the name Matsumae, received a charter from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> in 1604 granting his family the exclusive right to trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ainu">Ainu</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Matsumae grew no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and lived on trade. Retainers received trading posts instead of land, later contracted out to merchants who exploited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ainu">Ainu</a> labour in fisheries. Ainu resentment fuelled the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shakushain">Shakushain</a> in 1669 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menashi-kunashir-rebellion">Kunashir-Menashi uprising</a> of 1789. Fearing Russian expansion, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> took direct control of Ezo from 1799 to 1821 and again from 1855.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1868 forces of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-ezo">Republic of Ezo</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsumae-castle">Fukuyama</a> castle, completed in 1854 as the last traditional castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Restored in 1869 and renamed Tate, the domain was abolished in 1871 and absorbed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hokkaido">Hokkaido</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/matsumae-domain.jpg" alt="Matsumae Domain" width="320" height="429"><figcaption>Image: Fg2 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MatsumaeJo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1604 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Matsumae (Fukuyama)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Matsumae Yoshihiro, Matsumae Takahiro</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Matsumae han, Fukuyama Domain</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1738&amp;focus=matsumae-domain" aria-label="Matsumae Domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8098 701 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Matsumae Domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8418 957L8530 1145M8418 957L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8530 1145h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8418 957h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8418 957h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Matsumae. <a href="/atlas?year=1738&amp;focus=matsumae-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
